261
00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.950
Do you want a sip of tea or something?

262
00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:11.490
Because I will.

263
00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:16.330
I have made myself a wonderful iced tea because Lip Dub is about to happen.

264
00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:17.530
That's right.

265
00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:24.590
Our Fast Fact 3 is going to be breaking down that famous Lip Dub cold open.

266
00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:28.010
We got so many questions about this Lip Dub.

267
00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:30.210
I think we should just go through them one by one.

268
00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:34.170
We reached out to a lot of folks to get these answers for you.

269
00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:34.890
We did.

270
00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:35.670
We were busy.

271
00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:36.370
We were.

272
00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:37.050
It was fun.

273
00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:37.990
It was really fun.

274
00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:42.170
And I was also just so impressed with how quickly people got back to us.

275
00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:44.070
So, thank you to everyone we reached out to.

276
00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:44.970
Jenna, list them off.

277
00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:47.710
We reached out to Randy Cordray, of course.

278
00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:50.150
Our cinematographer, Matt Sohn.

279
00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:51.490
Our editor, Dave Rogers.

280
00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:52.390
Kate Flannery.

281
00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:53.330
Creed Bratton.

282
00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:57.390
And there was also a great breakdown with our writer, Danny Chun.

283
00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.890
And our first AD, Kelly Cantley, over on officetelly.com.

284
00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:03.830
And, Angela, you got some tidbits from the DVD commentary.

285
00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:07.650
So, without further ado, let's get started.

286
00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:09.070
Question number one.

287
00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:11.670
What was the inspiration for this cold open?

288
00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:15.670
Well, first off, Lip Dubs were very popular at this time.

289
00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:16.630
Crazy popular.

290
00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:21.290
The idea was, like, you and a group of friends, or a lot of times co-workers, actually.

291
00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:27.770
You would film yourself lip syncing to a song, and it was supposed to happen all in one take,

292
00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.850
but moved to various locations.

293
00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:30.590
Yeah.

294
00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:36.510
I remember one that was going around that everyone was watching was a bunch of office co-workers

295
00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.650
at a company called Connect Adventures.

296
00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:39.510
Oh, yeah.

297
00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:42.930
And they filmed it one night after work, and it was so fun.

298
00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:44.050
So, guess what?

299
00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:46.370
The Dunder Mifflin gang wants to get in on this.

300
00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:46.850
Mm-hmm.

301
00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:50.850
Dunder Mifflin's Lip Dub starts with Andy in the elevator.

302
00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:54.250
He sings Nobody But Me, performed by the Human Beans.

303
00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:57.310
And he dances to the front door of Dunder Mifflin.

304
00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:00.290
Someone flips the blinds to read Lip Dub.

305
00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:01.290
Who's that person?

306
00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:02.070
Someone did.

307
00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:02.930
That would be Pam.

308
00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:07.550
The door opens, and all of a sudden, everyone is dancing in the bullpen.

309
00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:09.570
Erin is on roller skates.

310
00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:15.190
It goes all the way through the kitchen into the break room and back, and it ends with confetti,

311
00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:20.530
and everyone participates pretty much except for Daryl, Angela, and Stanley barely participates

312
00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:21.230
until the end.

313
00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:25.570
And Ryan uses it as an opportunity to promote Woof.com.

314
00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:25.830
Right.

315
00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:31.830
I have to say, my favorite part of the Lip Dub is Kelly in front of the fan.

316
00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:32.670
In front of the fan.

317
00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:33.550
I just love it.

318
00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:38.010
I loved Kate strapped to Brian's back, but we'll get to that.

319
00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:38.370
Okay.

320
00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:44.310
Well, Danny Chun said the writers got the idea to do an office Lip Dub back in April or May

321
00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:47.830
before the summer break, before the end of season six.

322
00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:52.570
He said they'd been just watching Lip Dubs like we all were, but he thought,

323
00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:56.270
but what I'd love to see is one that's kind of full of mistakes.

324
00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:56.910
Yeah.

325
00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.010
And he said that felt like a fun idea to play with.

326
00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:04.010
But Danny said they kind of forgot about the idea for a while, but when it came time to

327
00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:06.350
pitch on the season premiere, Danny brought it up again.

328
00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:11.710
On the DVD commentary, Danny said that this cold open Lip Dub was one of the most fun things

329
00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:13.130
he's ever been a part of.

330
00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:17.830
He said he wrote it in one day, and it was originally supposed to start in the parking

331
00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:18.270
lot.

332
00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:21.550
And then it was going to come through the warehouse and end up in the office.

333
00:14:21.550 --> 00:14:26.550
But since none of those buildings are connected in real life, as you know, Jenna, they could

334
00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:27.570
not do it in one take.

335
00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:32.830
So our director, Jeff Blitz, suggested it all take place in the main office so that we

336
00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:35.090
could do that whole one take look.

337
00:14:35.570 --> 00:14:40.650
That leads me into our next question, which was, how many takes did you have to do?

338
00:14:40.750 --> 00:14:45.030
And was it really one take or was there some sneaky editing involved?

339
00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:49.370
Well, guys, this kind of depends on who you ask.

340
00:14:49.530 --> 00:14:50.750
This was very funny.

341
00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:54.010
Everyone's memory was a little different on this one.

342
00:14:54.210 --> 00:15:00.650
According to Danny Chun, it was one long continuous take, and we did it about three or four times.

343
00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:08.130
My memory was that it was one long take, and I remembered that we did three.

344
00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:10.270
Angela, what did you remember?

345
00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:15.310
I remembered that it was all one long take as well, because it was a domino effect of

346
00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:15.610
things.

347
00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:19.190
So I was there in Daryl's office, and I had to shut the door.

348
00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:22.930
But me shutting the door cued Andy to say, go, go, go.

349
00:15:23.110 --> 00:15:27.710
So I just remember all of these moments, and we did it all in one take.

350
00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:30.790
But I do remember that we got it pretty quickly.

351
00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:32.890
Like, we were impressed with ourselves.

352
00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:34.650
We were like, oh my gosh, we got that.

353
00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:35.870
Yeah, same memory.

354
00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:41.130
Well, when we reached out to cinematographer Matt Stone, he confirmed that we did do this

355
00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:43.150
as one long continuous take.

356
00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:49.710
However, he said that they did work in a couple of spots where we could cut if we needed to,

357
00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.990
but we did shoot it as one long take.

358
00:15:54.010 --> 00:16:00.250
So then, when we reached out to our editor, Dave Rogers, he actually went back and checked

359
00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:00.930
his files.

360
00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:03.750
He said we did five takes.

361
00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:05.590
And he sent them to us.

362
00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:06.590
Amazing.

363
00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:11.870
He said we used a combination of take four and take five.

364
00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:20.010
So he said it's mostly all take five, but we cut when Angela slams Daryl's office door,

365
00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:26.950
and then we cut on the whip to take four as the camera moves by Phyllis dancing and heads

366
00:16:26.950 --> 00:16:30.390
into Michael's office as he performs the magic tricks with his wand.

367
00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:35.330
And then we whip out back to take five as Andy yells, streamers.

368
00:16:35.530 --> 00:16:38.070
And then we stayed in take five until the end.

369
00:16:38.590 --> 00:16:43.510
And Dave said, looking at all the takes, he thinks the reason we did this was because

370
00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:50.630
Steve's magic in his office was best in take four, and they really wanted that magic take

371
00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:52.050
in the episode.

372
00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:57.650
I just have to give a big thank you to Dave Rogers because we all had different memories,

373
00:16:57.670 --> 00:17:00.070
and he had the answer.

374
00:17:00.290 --> 00:17:01.230
Yeah, thank you, Dave.

375
00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:07.050
Incidentally, in terms of Michael's magic, Danny Chun shared that they basically just

376
00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:11.170
gave Steve a bunch of dorky magic tricks and let Steve improvise it.

377
00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:16.590
So each of those magic takes in his office were a little bit different, except for the fire

378
00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:17.010
part.

379
00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:20.770
Yeah, they had a magician's helper named Amos Levkovich.

380
00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:25.410
He was hiding behind Michael's door to preset that flame trick at the end.

381
00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:27.890
So here were all of Michael's tricks.

382
00:17:28.210 --> 00:17:33.470
A dove comes out of his wand, flowers appear out of his cane, and then, of course, that

383
00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:34.670
flame in his hand.

384
00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:40.930
And I remember Steve loved that dove coming out of the wand, and he does it like three

385
00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:41.490
times.

386
00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:42.070
I know.

387
00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:43.190
It's so funny.

388
00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:44.830
All right, let's see.

389
00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:48.070
Next question, how did they choose which song to use?

390
00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:50.030
Well, that was all Danny.

391
00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:52.470
It was the only song he considered.

392
00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:58.390
He said he really wanted a song that was, quote, short, upbeat, fun to lip sync to, and

393
00:17:58.390 --> 00:17:59.430
not too current.

394
00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:04.170
Randy said it cost us $50,000 to get the song rights.

395
00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:08.370
And we should probably add that we could really hear that song.

396
00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:12.910
They had speakers, like, hidden all around the set, and it was really playing the song.

397
00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.870
We were really doing, like, lip syncing.

398
00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:15.230
Yeah.

399
00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:19.730
And I mean, I don't think you can fake that kind of energy that a song brings.

400
00:18:19.930 --> 00:18:22.730
I was so glad that they were playing it throughout the office.

401
00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:23.230
Yes.

402
00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:29.070
Our next questions are, who came up with the choreography, and what was the rehearsal process

403
00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:29.490
like?

404
00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:32.450
This was heavily choreographed, you guys.

405
00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:33.050
Mm-hmm.

406
00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:37.030
So, the lip dub was choreographed by Mary Ann Kellogg.

407
00:18:37.230 --> 00:18:39.410
She planned it out with a team of dancers.

408
00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:44.390
They shared on the DVD commentary that every single detail you see was planned.

409
00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:47.570
Randy Cordray even sent us their rehearsal video.

410
00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:49.870
I loved watching it, Jenna.

411
00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:50.450
Me too.

412
00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:53.190
They had all these dancers doing our part.

413
00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:55.170
Way better than we did them, by the way.

414
00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:55.370
Oh, yeah.

415
00:18:55.370 --> 00:18:56.250
Like, real dancers.

416
00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:57.710
They were amazing.

417
00:18:57.910 --> 00:18:59.930
The rehearsal dancers need a shout-out.

418
00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:06.330
They were Tammy Toe, Lauren Marie Menendez, Michael Cothran Penna, Jennifer Mimi Karsh,

419
00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:08.250
Aaron Cash, and John Todd.

420
00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:09.750
They were so great.

421
00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:11.350
We couldn't have done this without them.

422
00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:16.330
Randy Cordray said, we can put the rehearsal in our Insta stories so you guys can watch it.

423
00:19:16.330 --> 00:19:21.330
And Danny said they had scripted out all the beats, but there were little things that were tricky.

424
00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:25.510
For example, getting Aaron from reception to the kitchen in time,

425
00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:31.170
or the fact that he wrote in the script that Meredith is strapped to Kevin's back in the break room.

426
00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:31.430
Oh, yeah.

427
00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:32.690
He just wrote that down.

428
00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:33.030
Yeah.

429
00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:34.490
But how to make that happen.

430
00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:35.190
Exactly.

431
00:19:36.090 --> 00:19:39.310
Kelly Cantley shared that our special effects coordinator, Mike Thompson,

432
00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:41.910
built the harness that Kate had to wear.

433
00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:45.070
And you guys, I reached out to Kate because I was like,

434
00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:48.310
Kate, will you please tell me about being strapped to Brian's back?

435
00:19:48.310 --> 00:19:49.390
How did you do it?

436
00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:50.290
What was the process?

437
00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:51.810
Here's what she had to say.

438
00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:54.990
Hey, it's Kate Flannery.

439
00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:56.530
Okay, so this is the deal.

440
00:19:56.750 --> 00:20:00.370
I was on a harness on Brian's back.

441
00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:07.010
There was a table behind us so that I wasn't putting all my weight on him the entire time.

442
00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:10.290
So I would squat on the table, even though it was high up,

443
00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:12.810
just to get my weight off of his back.

444
00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:14.490
Like, don't break another actor.

445
00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:15.210
Don't do it.

446
00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:21.270
Anyway, so I'm on his back on this crazy harness,

447
00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:23.370
and then he turns around,

448
00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:29.130
and I have the word nobody on my stomach.

449
00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:31.870
Okay, the first time we do it, it's perfect.

450
00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:33.910
The whole thing was perfect.

451
00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:36.650
Like, you guys, there's no cut in a lip dub.

452
00:20:36.850 --> 00:20:38.290
It has to be all one take.

453
00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:40.510
So everybody has to get their part right.

454
00:20:40.690 --> 00:20:40.890
Everybody.

455
00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:41.630
And we did.

456
00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:47.730
Except that they felt like my nobody was too faded.

457
00:20:48.010 --> 00:20:50.710
The marker had, like, absorbed into my skin.

458
00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:53.290
So I feel like I let everybody down.

459
00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:55.090
So we had to do, we did,

460
00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:56.910
I think we did two more takes for safety,

461
00:20:56.910 --> 00:20:59.090
but I still feel like we would have had it on the first one

462
00:20:59.090 --> 00:21:00.270
if you could read my nobody.

463
00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:04.190
So my pasty white skin absorbed the Sharpie.

464
00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:08.970
It was, yeah, it took a couple days for it to come off.

465
00:21:08.990 --> 00:21:09.810
I'll be honest with you.

466
00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:12.630
It's kind of weird to look in the mirror

467
00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:16.210
when you're taking a shower or getting dressed,

468
00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:18.270
and your stomach says nobody.

469
00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:20.870
Ugh, don't try this at home, folks.

470
00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:26.110
But she said that nobody,

471
00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:28.250
they had to go back and ride it over

472
00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:30.210
and, you know, really darken it.

473
00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:32.370
And it was there for days.

474
00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:34.130
Well, I loved this day.

475
00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:36.930
I remember we came in early and we rehearsed,

476
00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:38.890
and then we went to hair and makeup.

477
00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:41.130
And I remember Randy told us we had the whole day.

478
00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:43.090
We should just take the day.

479
00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:44.150
Right, no stress.

480
00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:45.930
Yeah, and all the dancers were there.

481
00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:50.010
We watched their video, and then whoever was dancing our part

482
00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:51.370
would show us our part.

483
00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:52.490
And show us the timing.

484
00:21:52.790 --> 00:21:53.870
Mm-hmm, yes.

485
00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:55.710
So Randy told me that, yes,

486
00:21:55.850 --> 00:21:57.850
we started rehearsing at 7.15 in the morning.

487
00:21:57.850 --> 00:22:00.610
We got our first take at 11.23.

488
00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:03.670
That's the precision of Randy Cordray.

489
00:22:03.790 --> 00:22:05.090
He knows the exact moment.

490
00:22:05.370 --> 00:22:07.110
He's like an air traffic controller.

491
00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:08.910
Your flight will leave at 7.12.

492
00:22:09.170 --> 00:22:10.070
Yeah, exactly.

493
00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:11.430
Okay, all right, Randy.

494
00:22:11.870 --> 00:22:14.650
And we wrapped for the day at 1.36.

495
00:22:14.650 --> 00:22:16.570
6, and it was just so much fun.

496
00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:18.770
That's an early wrap day for us, you guys.

497
00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:20.210
So that meant we had the whole afternoon.

498
00:22:20.230 --> 00:22:21.430
We were probably so excited.

499
00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:22.110
I know.

500
00:22:22.390 --> 00:22:25.170
I remember our biggest hurdle was that there was a big cleanup

501
00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:26.490
between each take.

502
00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:27.050
Right, the confetti.

503
00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:28.730
With all the streamers and everything.

504
00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:32.150
But besides that, we were able to really go through those takes

505
00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:32.830
pretty quickly.

506
00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:35.230
Angela, I got a fan question.

507
00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:35.730
Mm-hmm.

508
00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:40.870
From Ian Oh in South Africa and Diana T in Paris, France

509
00:22:40.870 --> 00:22:45.870
to international folks who are wondering,

510
00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:49.250
Angela, were you bummed that you couldn't really participate

511
00:22:49.250 --> 00:22:52.150
